  "notes": "Finnish trance/Eurodance instrumental, and the research calls it \"the most-documented single production in this whole section\" — tempo, key, both studio signal chains, the DAW/tracker lineage, the production timeline and the origin of the title are all documented, which is why this is one of the section's two flagships and the only one rated `high`. Two numbers are facts rather than readings: **136 BPM in E minor**, so the band is narrow and E minor carries the top key weight — ±2 around 136, the same playing room the section's other point-tempo entries get (Deadmau5 at 128, Skrillex at 140). The defining parameter is stated as an inversion of how melody normally works: \"a staccato, gated, tightly repeated monosynth lead figure in 16ths/8ths that functions percussively — melody-as-drum-pattern\". That is the highest melody density of any producer model here at 10-16 notes a bar, staccato, in a narrow band, with the octave-jump probability raised because the entry names \"octave displacement of one figure\" as one of only three devices the record uses (the others being restatement and filtering). The harmony is \"a static single-chord-per-8-bars harmonic field — the record's motion is rhythmic and timbral, not harmonic\", which is exactly what `chords.impliedByRiff` describes: the lead states the harmony and the chord part is a support, not a progression. The kit is trance grammar with a long-tailed kick, hard clap on 2&4, offbeat open hat and 16th closed hats, with snare-roll builds as the primary transition device and percussion \"deliberately plain so the staccato lead reads as the rhythm section\" — a 0-1 perc bed. The bass is \"sub-and-saw locked to the kick on straight 8ths, ducked under it; the lead's low octave doubles as bass information\". The arrangement has **no vocal at all**. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the band width around the documented 136, the velocity tiers, the roll ramp and the section lengths — the research says only the bar-level arrangement is inferred, and this model marks the same boundary. ⚠ **`bassline.rhythm` is `boom_chick`, not `mirror_kick`** — the research names the note value: *“no 808. Sub-and-saw bass locked to the kick on straight 8ths, ducked under it; the lead's low octave doubles as bass information”*. Mirroring a four-on-the-floor kick writes quarter notes rather than the straight 8ths the entry describes; `boom_chick` places the 8th-note pulse and still lands on every kick.",
